Jakob Creutzig <creutzig@mathematik.tu-darmstadt.de> wrote:
>AFAIK, MR doesn't help against the death ray.  Rodney surely 
>has MR, but is easily fingered out.  

The property of monsters that is often called "magic resistance" should
really be called "saving throw".  No saving throw applies to death rays;
either the victim is vulnerable, in which case an unreflected death ray
hitting them will kill them instantly, or they are not, in which case an
unreflected death ray hitting them has no effect.

[here, I use "unreflected" to refer only to the current victim not
having reflection, of course.]

Rodney has a phenomenal saving throw, but does not have magic resistance
as is granted by GDSM, artifacts, or CoMRs.  Furthermore, he is neither
undead nor demonic nor unliving (e.g. golems).  Hence, he has no natural 
immunity to death magic.  Hence, death rays kill him.  Wearing a cloak
of magic resistance or GDSM, or possessing one of the quest artifacts
that grants magic resistance when carried, will prevent death rays from
killing him. (This amplifies the risk of depending on your class's quest
artifact for magic resistance; if Rodney steals it, not only are you now
not magic resistant, but Rodney *becomes* magic resistant so you'll have
to stone him, get him swallowed, or beat him to death with your weapon
in order to get your quest artifact back.)

Wizard player characters start the game with a cloak of magic resistance,
and hence are not harmed by death rays (unless they've removed their
cloak, or had it destroyed by a scroll or by being polymorphed by a ring
or a potion or a mouthful of toxic waste, or had it stolen by a nymph or
monkey).
